---
import InteriorLayout from '../../layouts/InteriorLayout.astro';
import StarDivider from '../../components/StarDivider.astro';
import PageWayfinding from '../../components/PageWayfinding.astro';
---
<InteriorLayout title='Academic Coaching | Raya Maia' description='Academic coaching built around the learner: practical support for strategy, writing, executive function, confidence, and independence.' pageClass='page-coaching' canonicalPath='/work/coaching/'>
<main>
<div class="page-shell">
<PageWayfinding position="top" />
<section class="sky-hero">
<div class="sky-hero-copy reveal">
<p class="kicker">
              Academic Coaching
            </p>
<h1>
              Support that builds capability, not dependence.
            </h1>
<p class="lede">
              I work with students at all levels who need more than someone to explain the next question — including those 
			  navigating learning disabilities, executive-function challenges, academic setbacks, or simply a way 
			  of learning that doesn’t fit neatly into the standard approach. 
			  <br/><br/>Together, we figure out where the 
			  process is breaking down and build the strategies, systems, and confidence to move forward.
            </p>
</div>
</section>
<StarDivider />
<section class="field-section">
<div class="field-layout">
<aside class="field-index reveal">
<strong>01</strong>Method
            </aside>
<div class="field-content reveal">
<h2>
                The student is the system.
              </h2>
<p>
                There is no perfect planner, study method, explanation, or colour-coded calendar waiting to save 
				everyone. Good support begins by understanding the individual learner: how they process information, 
				what gets in the way, what already works, and what they’re actually trying to accomplish.
              </p>
<div class="method-grid coaching-method">
<div>
<p class="micro-label">
                    01 / Understand
                  </p>
<h3>
                    Find the actual point of friction.
                  </h3>
<p>
                    “I’m bad at science” can have a dozen different meanings. Maybe the concepts aren’t clicking. 
					Maybe the student understands them but can’t retrieve them on a test. Maybe they have plenty 
					of ideas for an essay but no idea how to organize them. Or maybe getting started takes longer 
					than doing the work itself.
					<br><br>Before trying to fix the problem, we figure out what the problem actually is.
                  <br><br></p>
</div>
<div>
<p class="micro-label">
                    02 / Design
                  </p>
<h3>
                    Build around the learner.
                  </h3>
<p>
                    Once we know where the friction is, we can build around it. That might mean breaking 
					a large assignment into concrete steps, redesigning a study routine, creating a system for 
					tracking deadlines, finding a different way into a difficult concept, preparing for a 
					conversation with a teacher, or turning “I have no idea where to start” into something actionable.
                  </p>
</div>
<div>
<p class="micro-label">
                    03 / Transfer
                  </p>
<h3>
                    Make the strategy portable.
                  </h3>
<p>
                    A system that only works while I’m sitting beside you isn’t a very good system. I explain why 
					we’re trying a strategy, involve students in adjusting it, and gradually hand more of the process 
					back to them. The aim is for students to leave with approaches they can recognize, adapt, and use 
					when the next unfamiliar problem shows up.
                  </p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="field-section">
<div class="field-layout">
<aside class="field-index reveal">
<strong>02</strong>Support
            </aside>
<div class="field-content reveal">
<h2>
                Flexible help for complex academic lives.
              </h2>
<div class="observation-list">
<div class="observation">
<span class="dot"></span>
<div>
<h3>
                      Academic strategy & executive function
                    </h3>
<p>
                      Planning assignments, managing deadlines, studying effectively, initiating tasks, 
					  organizing materials, preparing for tests, and recovering when the plan inevitably goes sideways.
                    </p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="observation">
<span class="dot"></span>
<div>
<h3>
                      Subject support
                    </h3>
<p>
                      Concept review and problem-solving across academic subjects, with explanations adapted to 
					  how the student learns rather than repeated louder in increasingly desperate ways.
                    </p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="observation">
<span class="dot"></span>
<div>
<h3>
                      Writing & communication
                    </h3>
<p>
                      Turning scattered ideas into structured writing, developing and revising assignments, 
					  interpreting expectations, planning presentations, and figuring out what you 
					  actually want to say and how to say it clearly.
                    </p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="observation">
<span class="dot"></span>
<div>
<h3>
                      Confidence & self-advocacy
                    </h3>
<p>
                      Helping students understand what works for them, recognize where they need support, ask 
					  useful questions, make use of accommodations, and take an increasingly active role in 
					  their own education.
                    </p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<PageWayfinding position="bottom" />
</div>
</main>
</InteriorLayout>
